window.PORTFOLIO_DETAILS={"molds": {"tag": "PERSONAL FRAMEWORK · IN DEVELOPMENT", "title": "The MOLDS checklist", "body": "<p>A small checklist I made to think more clearly before sending a complicated request to ChatGPT.</p><h3>Five things to consider</h3><p><strong>Material:</strong> What information does ChatGPT need?<br><strong>Outcome:</strong> What are you trying to get done?<br><strong>Limits:</strong> What restrictions or requirements matter?<br><strong>Done when:</strong> What would make the result good enough?<br><strong>Shape:</strong> What should the final answer look like?</p><p>Not every request needs all five. “What is evaporation?” is already a clear request. The checklist is useful when the task has more moving parts.</p>"}, "arif": {"tag": "CREATOR PROJECT · IN DEVELOPMENT", "title": "Building arif4you", "body": "<p>arif4you is my creator project about technology, productivity, and practical AI. The focus is on useful explanations and honest demonstrations.</p><h3>The idea</h3><p>Show a real problem, explain what changes, and give viewers something they can try themselves. Practical prompting is one starting point, including examples built around the MOLDS checklist.</p><p>The project is taking shape. This portfolio will grow alongside the content.</p>"}, "textile": {"tag": "INDUSTRIAL VISIT · LEARNING EXPERIENCE", "title": "Inside a composite knitting mill", "body": "<p>An industrial visit to GMS Composite Knitting Ind. Ltd. in Gazipur offered a closer look at textile production beyond the classroom.</p><h3>What I observed</h3><p>The visit covered the mill’s production sections, including knitting and dyeing. Machinery included single jersey, double jersey, and Jacquard knitting machines.</p><h3>The takeaway</h3><p>Seeing connected production stages helps make sense of the relationship between fabric structure, processing, and the final product.</p>"}, "note1": {"tag": "FIELD NOTE · PROMPTING", "title": "A checklist, not a magic spell.", "body": "<p>A prompt is a request. Its job is to give enough information for a useful response. Making it longer is not automatically an improvement.</p><p>For a simple definition, the subject may be enough. For exam notes, the course material, expected depth, and answer format can change the result substantially.</p><p>MOLDS helps you notice those missing decisions. Start with the outcome, add the context that affects it, and stop when the request is clear. You do not need to fill every box.</p>"}, "note2": {"tag": "FIELD NOTE · LEARNING", "title": "Start with the thing people can use.", "body": "<p>A helpful explanation should leave someone able to do something: ask a clearer question, compare two options, or understand why a process works.</p><p>One way to get there is to start with an everyday problem. Show the first attempt, make one meaningful change, and explain the result.</p><p>Examples do more than make a lesson interesting. They let people see where an idea applies and decide whether it helps them.</p>"}, "note3": {"tag": "FIELD NOTE · TEXTILES", "title": "Look beyond the finished garment.", "body": "<p>A garment is the visible end of a much longer process. Fibre choice, yarn structure, fabric construction, dyeing, finishing, and assembly all influence its properties.</p><p>A comfortable shirt is not explained by a single specification. Its feel and performance come from decisions made across the production chain.</p><p>Following those connections is part of learning textile engineering: look at the finished product, then work backwards through the choices that made it possible.</p>"}, "smart": {"tag": "2025 · WET PROCESSING II LAB · ACADEMIC PROJECT", "title": "pH-sensitive smart textile using natural dye", "body": "<p>Used roselle-derived anthocyanin on cotton to create a reversible colour response across acidic, neutral, and alkaline conditions. The work explored mordant concentration and thermal processing to improve wash-fastness performance, with full documentation of the method, observations, and analysis.</p><p>A cotton fabric designed to visibly respond to changes in its chemical environment, using anthocyanin extracted from roselle.</p><h3>What the project explored</h3><p>The fabric showed a reversible colour response across acidic, neutral, and alkaline environments. The lab work examined how mordant concentration and thermal processing could improve wash-fastness performance.</p><h3>From testing to documentation</h3><p>The work included colour-response testing and a complete experimental record covering the methodology, observations, and analysis.</p><p>This was a coursework project for the Wet Processing II Lab in 2025.</p>"}, "survey": {"tag": "2025 · GROUP RESEARCH · ACADEMIC PROJECT", "title": "Sustainable fashion awareness survey", "body": "<p>Worked with a student team to examine awareness of sustainable fashion and consumer behaviour. I helped collect and organize 130+ survey responses and contributed to the report and presentation of findings.</p><p>A student group research activity examining awareness of sustainable fashion and patterns in consumer behaviour.</p><h3>My contribution</h3><p>I helped collect and organize more than 130 survey responses for analysis and reporting, and contributed to the written report and presentation of findings.</p><h3>The focus</h3><p>The project connected sustainability questions with consumer perspectives, turning a collection of survey responses into material the team could analyze and communicate.</p>"}};
